interface_contract:
NEW CLI SURFACE on the `teardown` verb of `scripts/cmux/dispatch.mjs` (backend owns this; the only current consumer is a hand-typed orchestrator invocation, and contingently a future chain-side caller):

    node scripts/cmux/dispatch.mjs teardown --task <slug> [--outcome ok|refused] [--keep-artifacts]

NEW EXPORTED CONSTANTS from `scripts/cmux/dispatch.mjs` (both frozen, both pinned by a drift-guard test):

    export const TEARDOWN_OUTCOMES = Object.freeze(['ok', 'refused'])
    export const DEFAULT_TEARDOWN_OUTCOME = 'ok'

RESOLUTION + VALIDATION CONTRACT (first statements in `teardownCmd`, before any side effect):

    // absent => DEFAULT_TEARDOWN_OUTCOME ('ok'), preserving today's behaviour
    // byte-for-byte. Present-but-out-of-enum REFUSES — never coerced, never
    // defaulted on a typo. Hoisted above the surface-closing block below so a
    // bad flag closes nothing and deletes nothing.
    const outcome = args.outcome ?? DEFAULT_TEARDOWN_OUTCOME
    if (!TEARDOWN_OUTCOMES.includes(outcome)) {
      throw new UsageError(`teardown: --outcome must be one of ${TEARDOWN_OUTCOMES.join('|')}, got ${JSON.stringify(args.outcome)}`)
    }

CALL-SITE CONTRACT (replaces `dispatch.mjs:2411`):

    const archive = Boolean(args['keep-artifacts']) || shouldArchive({ outcome }, records)

UNCHANGED CONSUMED CONTRACTS: `shouldArchive(task, dispatches) -> boolean` from `scripts/cmux/contract.mjs:253` (task-level `outcome !== 'ok'` ⇒ archive; fail-closed on a missing/undefined/null outcome). `UsageError` → exit 2 via `main()`. `teardownCmd`'s RETURN SHAPE IS UNCHANGED: `{ code: 0, json: { task_dir, state_dir, leftover_worktrees } }` — no new key is added to the JSON, so nothing downstream needs updating.

SEMANTICS: `--outcome refused` is the caller asserting "the overall work was refused" and forces archival of both dirs; `--outcome ok` (or absent) leaves archival decided solely by the dispatch records, exactly as today; `--keep-artifacts` still forces archival independently of `--outcome` (it short-circuits the `||` before `shouldArchive` is even called). Archival is the fail-safe direction throughout.
